items.statement:
Work in the two-dimensional simple $U_q(\mathfrak{sl}_2)$-module $V(\omega)=L_1(1)$ with basis $v$ (weight $\omega$) and $w=F_1v$ (weight $\omega-\alpha_1$), so that $\tilde f_1v=w$, $\tilde e_1w=v$, $\tilde e_1v=0$, $\tilde f_1w=0$ and $\mathcal L(\omega)=A_0v\oplus A_0w$ is a crystal lattice with crystal basis $\{v+q\mathcal L(\omega),\,w+q\mathcal L(\omega)\}$ ([[thm-quantized-sl-two-string-formulas]](iii), [[def-kashiwara-lattices-lambda-and-infinity]], [[def-crystal-lattice-and-crystal-basis]]).

Let $$L:=A_0(v+w)\oplus A_0(qv)\subseteq V(\omega).$$ Then:
(a) $L$ is a free $A_0$-module of rank $2$ and generates $V(\omega)$ over $\mathbb Q(q)$;
(b) $L$ is not stable under $\tilde e_1$: $\tilde e_1(v+w)=v\notin L$;
(c) $L$ is not weight-decomposed: $L_\omega=L\cap\mathbb Q(q)v=A_0qv$ and $L_{\omega-\alpha_1}=L\cap\mathbb Q(q)w=qA_0w$, but $v+w\notin L_\omega\oplus L_{\omega-\alpha_1}$, so $L\ne L_\omega\oplus L_{\omega-\alpha_1}$.

Consequently there is no set $B$ for which $(L,B)$ is a crystal basis of $V(\omega)$: a crystal basis requires $L$ to be a crystal lattice, i.e. to satisfy [[def-crystal-lattice-and-crystal-basis]] (i)-(iii), and (b) violates (iii) while (c) violates (ii). In particular the axioms (ii) and (iii) are not automatic for a free lattice generating the module. No choice principle is used. The two lattice axioms are independent in the class of allowed graded quantum data. For weight decomposition without operator stability in the same L_1(1), use L'=A0(qv)⊕A0w: ẽw=v∉L'. For operator stability without weight decomposition, take rank-one data P=Z omega⊕Z delta, Pvee=Z h⊕Z d, pairing <h,omega>=<d,delta>=1 and other basis pairings0, alpha=2omega, simple coroot h, d_1=1. On the direct sum of the one-dimensional modules of labels0 and delta all E/F and Kashiwara operators vanish, so A0(v+w)⊕A0(qv) is stable under them but is not label-weight decomposed. Thus neither axiom follows from the other for general crystal lattices. These two additional explicit witnesses justify the original independence clause; the first mixed lattice alone did not.
